# Chaiss LLM configuration — a literate example

This file is both documentation and a working configuration: cola is
markdown-native, so the fenced `cola` block below is parsed and the
prose around it is ignored. To use it:

    cp .chaiss.cola.example .chaiss.cola

then replace each `your_key_here` with a real API key (a provider whose
key is left as the placeholder is treated as not configured). The
resulting `.chaiss.cola` is gitignored, and it may stay exactly this
literate — keep the prose, edit the block. One cola quirk: a prose line
may not *begin* with a backtick, so keep inline code away from the
start of a line.

## How chaiss reads it

- Every provider below with a real `api_key` appears in the model
  dropdown of the chat pane (shown when more than one is configured).
- `backend` names the provider that starts selected at launch:
  `google`, `openai`, `anthropic`, or `ollama`.
- `model` overrides that provider's built-in default; omit the field to
  keep the default (google: `gemini-3.7-flash`, openai: `gpt-6-astra`,
  anthropic: `claude-3-opus-20240229`).
- `.chaiss.cola` takes precedence over `.env` **per key**: anything not
  set here falls back to the `.env` values (`GOOGLE_API_KEY`,
  `LLM_MODEL_OPENAI`, ...), so both files can coexist.

## The configuration

```cola
llm:
    backend: "google"
    provider plural providers:
        google:
            api_key: "your_key_here",
            model: "gemini-3.7-flash"
        ;
        openai:
            api_key: "your_key_here",
            model: "gpt-6-astra"
        ;
        anthropic:
            api_key: "your_key_here",
            model: "claude-3-opus-20240229"
        ;
    ;
;
```

## Notes

- Ollama needs no API key; it is offered only when `backend` (or
  `LLM_BACKEND` in `.env`) is set to `ollama`.
- This example is parsed by a unit test in `chaiss-core`, so it can
  never drift from what the application actually understands.
